 ...         print(line)
 ```
 
-HTTPX will use universal line endings, normalising all cases to `\n`.
+By default, `iter_lines()` removes line endings, just like `str.splitlines()`.
+Passing `keepends=False` explicitly has the same behavior. Use `keepends=True`
+to retain each original line ending, including `\r\n`, `\r`, `\n`, and the other
+separators recognized by `str.splitlines()`:
+
+```pycon
+>>> r = httpx.Response(200, text="first\r\nsecond\rthird\nlast")
+>>> list(r.iter_lines(keepends=True))
+['first\r\n', 'second\r', 'third\n', 'last']
+```
+
+Both modes work with cached and streaming responses, yielding lines incrementally.
+An empty response yields no lines, and a final line without an ending is returned
+unchanged. The async equivalent is `response.aiter_lines(keepends=True)`.
 
 In some cases you might want to access the raw bytes on the response without applying any HTTP content decoding. In this case any content encoding that the web server has applied such as `gzip`, `deflate`, `brotli`, or `zstd` will
 not be automatically decoded.
